# XSH 2.8 Realtime
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# The concepts of the realtime sections that no function page already
# tests. Signals (2.8.1) are in XSH.2.4.2-realtime-signals; asynchronous
# I/O, memory mapping, scheduling interfaces and timers are mostly tested
# with their functions, and the .cov file points there.

assertion  XSH.2.8-realtime.001
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.8.3
class      required
says       A store through a shared mapping to a byte that lies beyond the end
           of the file but inside the last mapped page does not change the
           size of the file (fstat() gives the old st_size before and
           after).
note       Whole pages beyond the end give SIGBUS (XSH.2.4.3-signal-actions.021);
           this is the part inside the last page. The store is not required
           to be kept.
test       t/001-size-unaffected.c

assertion  XSH.2.8-realtime.002
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.8.5
class      required
says       The finest resolution of CLOCK_REALTIME and of CLOCK_MONOTONIC,
           as clock_getres() reports it, is not coarser than 20 ms, and
           {_POSIX_CLOCKRES_MIN} is 20000000 (nanoseconds) in <limits.h>.
uses       XSH.clock_getres.001
test       t/002-clock-resolution.c

assertion  XSH.2.8-realtime.003
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.8.3
class      required
says       Two processes that map the same part of a file with MAP_SHARED see
           each other's stores: a byte written by a child through its mapping
           is seen by the parent through its own (no msync() between).
test       t/003-shared-file-mapping-visible.c

assertion  XSH.2.8-realtime.004
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.8.3
class      conditional
when       SHM
says       Two processes that map the same shared memory object see each
           other's stores: a byte written by a child through its mapping
           of an object from shm_open() is seen by the parent through its
           own.
test       t/004-shared-object-visible.c

assertion  XSH.2.8-realtime.005
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.8.3
class      conditional
when       MLR+XSI|MLR+SIO
since      2001
says       A memory lock a process holds on a page of a shared file
           mapping is not held by a child made by fork() (the child's own
           msync() with MS_INVALIDATE over that page does not fail with
           EBUSY while the parent's does), and unmapping the page removes
           the lock: a fresh mapping of the same file part at the same
           address is not locked.
note       Lock state is seen through msync() with MS_INVALIDATE failing
           with EBUSY over locked pages, as the mlock() module does.
           Whether locks also vanish across exec and process termination
           is not tested.
uses       XSH.msync.004
test       t/005-lock-fork-unmap.c
